WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
If you have too many interests, people

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.520
treat you like you're broken. Everyone

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.040
else looks certain. They picked a lane,

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.640
they're getting promoted, [music]

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.120
and you feel unfinished. A jack of all

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.320
trades, a master [music] of none. But,

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.000
what if your lack of focus isn't your

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:23.000
weakness, but a weapon? I have been a

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.320
CEO, board member, and investor [music]

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.960
in tech companies that created billions

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.840
in value. I was also an MIT grad, a

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:33.640
musician, a monk in training, and a

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:36.240
homeless teenager. So, on paper, I was a

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.840
mess. I thought those chapters were

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.280
random detours. [music] They weren't. In

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.280
this video, I'll show you why having too

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.960
many interests is your biggest career

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.200
advantage, and offer a path for those of

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.240
us who were never meant to be just one

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:54.160
thing. First, the old advice, pick one

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:57.120
lane and specialize, is getting riskier

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.040
by the month. The world has changed. If

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.360
you're looking to grow professionally,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.440
financially, [music] personally, you

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.400
know how it feels right now. Since you

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.080
were a kid, you've been told to pick a

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.920
lane, [music]

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.720
get really good at one thing, and then

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:14.520
wait your turn. But, today, a single

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.720
lane feels like a dead [music] end. AI

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.240
is eating a big chunk of all the junior

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:23.280
level work. Analysts, [music] junior

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.560
coders, entry-level researchers, the

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.640
ground floor that helped you build

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:30.680
expertise, is sinking. So, nowadays,

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.640
your multiple interests aren't a

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:36.520
liability anymore. They're your rope to

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:39.040
get out. Just ask the man people call

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.040
the grandfather of modern AI, Geoffrey

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.880
Hinton. He did not have a computer

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.000
science degree. He started with

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:49.160
experimental psychology. In the 1980s,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.720
the mainstream AI was dominated by logic

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:54.600
and rules and heuristics and expert

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:57.040
systems, but [music] Hinton was obsessed

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.920
with human brains, neurons, and

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.760
biological feedback. He asked a simple

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:05.200
question from an unrelated world. What

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.920
if a machine learned the way human

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.600
brains do? That became the root of the

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.680
modern AI revolution. Hinton was not a

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:16.896
specialist deep in the field of AI. He

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.560
brought a different obsession, and it

00:02:16.896 --> 00:02:20.960
[clears throat] changed the field. Larry

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.720
Page succeeded by going deep in computer

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.440
science. Steve Jobs succeeded after

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.000
dropping out of college, taking a

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.320
calligraphy class, and studying Zen.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.040
Both changed the world of technology.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.400
George Lucas went to film school. James

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.760
Cameron was a truck driver who taught

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.440
himself filmmaking. Both changed the

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.440
world of movies. Some were focused, some

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.720
looked scattered, some picked a single

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:47.760
lane, some had too many interests. There

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.320
is no single template. No one should

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.921
tell you that there is only one way to

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.480
change the world. Your own way is

00:02:52.921 --> 00:02:58.200
[music] the only way, because almost all

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:01.160
innovation is never brand new. It's a

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.600
recombination. Old ideas with new

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:05.440
neighbors. And in that world, if you

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.760
could see many dots, you could draw a

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:10.360
map that most specialists would miss.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.600
And in the age of AI, that map isn't

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.080
just your advantage, it's the difference

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.560
between impact [music]

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.600
and irrelevance. Here's the illusion.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.100
The best careers look like a straight

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.840
line, but most real careers are a maze.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.840
[music]

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.960
You know, for years, I carried resume

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:30.080
shame. The shame [music] of being told I

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.760
lacked focus, that I jumped around, that

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.000
I didn't finish what [music] I started.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.040
All true. While everyone around me

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.320
picked a lane and stayed in [music] it,

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:40.737
I kept switching. Monk training,

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.760
musician, math, tech,

00:03:40.737 --> 00:03:44.040
>> [music]

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:47.040
>> banking, C-suite, boards, investor. My

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:49.907
peers they go well a mile deep, and I

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.040
felt I was trading in a lake 6 in deep.

00:03:49.907 --> 00:03:54.120
[music] And you keep telling yourself,

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:56.000
"Something must be wrong with me." There

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.800
isn't. You're not scattered, [music]

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:01.042
you're unsorted. David Epstein studied

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.160
top performers across sports, science,

00:04:01.042 --> 00:04:05.560
[music] business, and art, and found

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:08.800
something very uncomfortable. In complex

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:11.360
fields, range beats early specialization

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:15.040
more often than we admit. That is the

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:17.840
M-shape. It's not one narrow pillar,

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.720
it's two or more genuine interests

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.560
connected by a bridge of curiosity. It's

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.680
the most resilient shape there is. If

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:28.800
one pillar collapses, the other holds.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:30.880
But, there is a deeper truth here. A

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.138
finance professional, for example, who

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.840
picks up photography, isn't M-shaped.

00:04:33.138 --> 00:04:36.440
[music]

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.960
Those worlds don't feed each other. They

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:42.200
just compete for the same hours. The

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:44.800
most resilient M-shape is a stack where

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:47.280
your interests multiply each other. To

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.040
find that connection, you need a filter.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.640
That's our next point. Here's a

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.600
framework to build multiple interests

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.840
that are actually reinforcing each

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:58.840
other. Here's a four-question test that

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.760
you can run in just 60 seconds. Take any

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:04.160
two interests, A and B. Ask four

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:07.200
questions. If the answer is yes, give it

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:11.120
one point. If not, then zero. I call it

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:13.748
the link test. L is language. Do you

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.440
ever describe A using B's language?

00:05:13.748 --> 00:05:18.320
[music]

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:21.960
I is for improve. When A improves, does

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:24.640
B get better without effort? N is need.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:28.280
Do you need the tools from A to solve

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.760
B's problems? And C is cut. If you cut A

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:33.360
entirely, would B get worse? If the

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:35.280
score is one or two, they're adjacent,

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.760
they're neighbors, but not linked

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.120
partners. If you get a zero, these two

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:41.440
interests are competing for your time,

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.920
not compounding. [music]

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.760
Now, if this is too abstract, let me run

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:48.000
this framework with two of my own

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:50.440
interests, just as an example. Years

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.160
ago, I trained as [music] a monk, A.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:54.840
Years later, I became the CEO of one of

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.920
the fastest-growing tech companies in

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:58.840
the US, B. [music] Now, they seem like

00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:02.000
they're not connected, but let's apply

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:05.800
the link framework and find out. Now, L

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.240
for language. Did I describe B using A's

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:10.720
language? Not really. It's not a strong

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.000
connection. So, score there is zero.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.720
Improve. When one improved, [music] did

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.240
the other improve? Yes, indeed. More

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:21.440
contemplative practices made me more

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:24.000
patient, more present, more resilient,

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:26.560
more focused, more focused on others,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:29.740
all good leadership skills. So, A

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.280
improved B. That's a one. Third one is N

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:33.360
[music]

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:35.440
or need. Did I ever need tools from one

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.680
to solve problems in the other?

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:42.080
Detachment from outcome is a negotiating

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:44.920
superpower. So, heck yeah, one there as

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:47.320
well. And finally, [music] C or cut. If

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:49.680
you removed monk training, would my

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:52.440
leadership be worse off? That's an easy

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.619
yes. So, one there. That's three out of

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.760
four. So, these two interests

00:06:54.619 --> 00:06:57.240
>> [music]

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:59.200
>> are linked. They reinforce each other.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:02.200
Now, from the outside, it looks

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:04.320
unrelated, but underneath is the same

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:06.800
engine. Now, this is just a random

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:09.400
example from my life, but you can apply

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.840
it to your interests to see which ones

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:13.760
are secretly working together to make

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:15.920
you successful. [music] The action item

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:18.760
is pretty straightforward. Pick any two

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:21.000
interests, run link, the total score

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:22.800
will tell you what stacks and reinforces

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.560
together. [music] You don't have to

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:28.120
invent the connection, just uncover it.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:30.680
Discovering which interests connect is

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:33.440
only half the problem. The harder

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:36.320
question is deciding which ones deserve

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:38.600
your focus right now. For that, you need

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:40.960
a sorting hat. In Harry Potter, the

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:42.760
sorting hat looked at every incoming

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:45.560
student and told them where they

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:48.200
belonged, which house. Your sorting hat

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:50.560
can sort your interests. Four questions.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:53.360
What am I already good at? What buys me

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.760
stability? What buys me optionality?

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.440
What restores me? If the interest does

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:01.400
not fall into one of these four

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:04.280
categories, it has no job in life and in

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:07.800
our careers, we go through seasons. We

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:09.880
change, we evolve. So do our interests.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:12.160
But, in this season that you're in right

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:14.600
now, each [music] interest that you have

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:16.920
must earn at least one of those four

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:19.880
jobs. Think about interests [music] like

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:22.390
stock options. They vest over time. Your

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:24.480
interests also need a vesting schedule.

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:26.975
[music] I'll again give you my personal

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.640
example. I'm not a good guitarist.

00:08:26.975 --> 00:08:30.480
[music]

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:32.320
I love playing, it's not my career. I

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.479
spent two years making sure that I could

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:36.479
play chords without thinking. My fingers

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:39.360
just know where to go now. But, [music]

00:08:36.479 --> 00:08:42.000
the job for that interest was the fourth

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.280
one. I play for 10-15 minutes when I

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.680
want to take a break. I love it, it

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:49.040
restores [music] me. That's the job. So,

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:51.360
two years later, I asked, "Do I want to

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:53.176
go pro level?" The answer was no,

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:56.040
because it would take years more.

00:08:53.176 --> 00:08:58.420
[music] So, for me, that interest was

00:08:56.040 --> 00:09:00.720
vested. I stopped investing, but I

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:03.080
[music] continue harvesting it for the

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.320
joy it gives me. Now, that's how I

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:07.920
thought about it. Now, you can run your

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:10.320
own sorting hat. Check if each of your

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.520
interests has at least one job to do.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:15.280
That is your focus map. You know,

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:18.440
starting early and staying in one lane

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:21.341
only works in a very specific kind of

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:23.400
world. We worship the Tiger Woods story.

00:09:21.341 --> 00:09:26.800
[music] Tiger was mimicking his dad's

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:28.600
swing as a toddler. 10,000 hours before

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.240
he was a teenager. That's the

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:33.840
conventional wisdom. Start early, narrow

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:37.320
quickly, become inevitable. Perfect

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:39.681
strategy for golf. Golf is a kind

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.800
environment. The rules never change,

00:09:39.681 --> 00:09:42.080
>> [music]

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:45.000
>> the feedback is instant and accurate,

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:46.880
repetition brings you rewards. But,

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:50.160
that's just one way. [music] Roger

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:53.160
Federer played tennis, soccer, squash,

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.360
wrestling, skiing. People thought he was

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:57.160
unfocused and distracted. [music] But,

00:09:55.360 --> 00:10:00.240
then he went on to become one of the

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:02.960
greatest tennis players in history. Why?

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:06.320
Because tennis is a wicked world. It's

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:09.120
adversarial and dynamic by design. Your

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:11.080
opponent adapts [music] in real time and

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:14.840
you have to make it up as you go.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:17.760
Business is wicked. Investing is wicked.

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:20.360
Medicine is wicked. Rules change,

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:23.280
patterns break, feedback arrives late or

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:25.532
never arrives or lies. Your growth is

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:26.080
about surviving this wicked world.

00:10:25.532 --> 00:10:28.880
[music]

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:31.560
Take Nokia. They dominated the world of

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:34.120
flip phones. Absolute experts. They

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:36.200
looked at the first iPhone and laughed.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:39.680
They thought, "No buttons. Who's going

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:41.400
to use this toy?" Their very expertise

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:43.560
had become [music] their blind spot.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:45.640
They were so deep inside their own

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:48.520
world, they couldn't see the next one

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:51.080
emerging. And today, AI is moving the

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:54.560
goal post before you even line up your

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:56.440
first kick. McKinsey projects that 50%

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:59.880
of work [music] activities could be

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:02.680
automated as early as 2030. What [music]

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:05.240
AI will dismantle is the ladder

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:07.480
underneath all of us. The ladder that

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:10.040
used to give you [music] reps so you

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:12.240
could become an expert, a specialist

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:14.680
down the line. So in your life and in

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:16.920
your career, if you dabbled in different

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:19.000
domains, you don't have to feel like

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:21.160
you're behind. You might be doing

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:23.839
exactly what you need to do. And the

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:27.480
data [music] is weirdly clear. Michigan

00:11:23.839 --> 00:11:29.400
State University studied 773 Nobel Prize

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:32.280
winners across a century. Nobel

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:35.120
scientists were 12 times more likely to

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:38.680
write fiction [music] or poetry. 22

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:40.858
times more likely to be actors, dancers,

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:41.120
or magic performers. So

00:11:40.858 --> 00:11:43.720
>> [music]

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:46.040
>> ask yourself, what if the interests that

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:48.600
you've collected across your life

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:50.560
weren't random? What if there is an

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:53.000
invisible thread [music]

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:55.520
that connects all of it? Find that

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:58.000
golden thread and your [music] interests

00:11:55.520 --> 00:12:00.160
stop competing with each other and start

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:02.480
compounding. Once you find that

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:04.760
invisible thread, something surprising

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:08.080
happens. You know, when I was doing

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:10.960
music full-time, I envied the power and

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:12.920
money that corporate jobs can bring you.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:16.000
And when I was on Wall Street, I envied

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:19.080
the freedom and beauty that musicians

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:21.680
can enjoy. I thought both were wrong

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:23.040
turns. They weren't. They were just

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:26.560
different seasons. [music]

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:28.839
And seasons don't have hard edges.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:33.160
There's no exact moment like [music]

00:12:28.839 --> 00:12:36.200
Tuesday at 8:45 a.m. when winter

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:37.640
suddenly becomes spring. They flow into

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:40.400
each other.

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:41.120
Your seasons are connected. Your

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:44.720
interests [music]

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:47.080
are connected. Nothing is wasted. In

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:50.000
Tao, there's a concept [music] of the

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:52.920
uncarved block. Once that block is

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:54.800
carved into a chair, it's useful, but

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:58.160
its potential is over. [music]

00:12:54.800 --> 00:13:01.400
From that point on, it can only be a

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:03.839
chair forever. But the uncarved block

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:04.600
isn't just one thing yet. It contains

00:13:03.839 --> 00:13:07.720
the potential [music]

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:10.320
to be so many things. So having too many

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:13.760
interests just means that you refuse to

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:16.440
be carved into a single limited tool.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:19.560
And if you ask me, the world already has

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:20.640
plenty of chairs. It needs more uncarved

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:23.360
blocks. [music]

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:26.160
It needs the painter, the priest, the

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:29.040
poet, the product manager. Because the

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:31.560
fabric of human progress is [music]

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:35.680
woven from all of them.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:38.280
One world intricately stitched together.

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:41.160
It needs all of you.

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:42.560
Especially the parts you were told to

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:44.680
hide.

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:46.839
If you enjoyed this video,

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:49.960
you'll love the next one. It's about how

00:13:46.839 --> 00:13:51.680
to master any skill so fast, it almost

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:52.839
feels unfair.

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:55.560
Thank you.

00:13:52.839 --> 00:13:55.560
And I love you.

